# Business Insights

This document records the most relevant business insights discovered during the project.

Insights are continuously updated as new analyses are completed.

---

# Insight 001 — Customer Consolidation

## Finding

Several customers were registered multiple times in the operational system.

## Business Impact

Using the customer registration ID fragmented customer purchasing history and produced inaccurate customer-level metrics and rankings.

## Action

Customers were consolidated using standardized Customer Name and CPF/CNPJ rather than the ERP customer registration ID.

---

# Insight 002 — Revenue and Profit Ranking

## Finding

After customer consolidation, the highest revenue customer was also identified as the highest profit customer.

## Business Impact

The initial analysis produced different conclusions because duplicate customer registrations split customer purchasing histories.

This demonstrated that customer-level profitability analysis depends directly on reliable customer identification.

---

# Insight 003 — Purchase Frequency and Customer Value

## Finding

Purchase frequency is strongly associated with customer economic value.

During the analyzed period:

- 627 customers (approximately 32% of the customer base) recorded only one purchase.
- These customers generated approximately 7% of total revenue.
- Customers with four or more purchases generated approximately 85% of total revenue.
- Customers with more than 21 purchases generated nearly 50% of total revenue.

However, some highly frequent customers generate considerably less profit than other customers with similar purchase frequency.

## Business Impact

Purchase frequency is an important indicator of customer value, but frequency alone does not provide a complete picture of customer profitability.

Because transaction history before August 2024 is unavailable due to ERP migration, customers with one recorded purchase cannot necessarily be classified as first-time customers.

## Recommendation

Frequency should be evaluated together with:

- Revenue
- Profit
- Profit margin
- Recency
- Product mix

---

# Insight 004 — Product Classification

## Finding

The original ERP product classification was too detailed for strategic analysis and contained duplicated or inconsistent product subgroup registrations.

## Business Impact

Commercial analyses became difficult to interpret, and counting technical subgroup IDs could overstate the number of distinct business categories purchased by a customer.

## Action

Original product subgroups were reorganized into broader Product Groups.

Business-level category metrics use normalized subgroup names where appropriate rather than relying exclusively on ERP technical IDs.

---

# Insight 005 — Data Quality

## Finding

Customer and product registration quality has a direct impact on analytical results.

Examples identified during the project include:

- Duplicate customer registrations
- Duplicate product subgroup registrations
- Products without an analytical Product Group classification

## Business Impact

Data quality problems may produce inaccurate:

- Revenue rankings
- Profit rankings
- Customer segmentation
- Product diversity metrics
- Inactivity analysis
- Product-level analysis

## Recommendation

Customer and product registration quality should be continuously monitored.

Unclassified products should also be monitored as a data quality KPI as new products are added to the ERP.

