# Insight 006 — Revenue Concentration

## Finding

Customer revenue is highly concentrated within a relatively small portion of the customer base.

- The top 20 customers account for approximately 21% of total revenue.
- Approximately 80% of total revenue is generated by 334 customers, representing about 17% of the 1,953 analyzed customers.
- Customers generating R$10,000 or more individually account for nearly 70% of total revenue.

## Business Impact

A relatively small group of customers has a disproportionate impact on company revenue.

However, the top 20 customers account for only 21% of revenue, indicating that revenue concentration extends across a broader group of valuable customers rather than depending exclusively on a few major accounts.

## Recommendation

Customer retention and relationship strategies should prioritize high-value customers while also identifying customers with the potential to move into higher-value segments.

---

# Insight 007 — Revenue Does Not Fully Represent Customer Value

## Finding

Revenue and profit are strongly related, but high revenue does not always translate proportionally into high profit.

The top 334 customers by revenue account for approximately 80% of total revenue but generate 75.15% of total profit.

Conversely, the top 391 customers by profit generate 80% of total profit and account for 82.47% of total revenue.

Among the 202 customers generating at least R$10,000 in revenue:

- 120 (59.4%) have profit margins of 25% or higher.
- Approximately 79% have margins of at least 20%.
- Only 14 customers have margins below 15%.
- Only two customers have margins below 10%.

## Business Impact

Revenue provides a strong indication of customer economic value, but differences in profitability remain relevant when prioritizing customers.

Most high-revenue customers also demonstrate healthy profitability, suggesting that low-margin high-revenue customers are the exception rather than the dominant pattern.

## Recommendation

Revenue should be used as the Monetary component of the RFM model.

Profit and Profit Margin should remain complementary customer-value measures and should be used to refine commercial prioritization.

