# Insight 010 — Low Frequency Does Not Necessarily Mean Low Customer Value

## Finding

Some low-frequency customers generated substantial revenue.

Thirty-six customers with three or fewer purchases generated approximately R$573,000 in revenue.

Most of the high-value, low-frequency customers purchased implements. Among the high-value customers with only one recorded purchase, the large majority purchased implements.

## Business Impact

Low purchase frequency may reflect the naturally longer replacement cycle of certain products rather than weak customer engagement.

A customer purchasing an implement may reasonably remain without another similar purchase for a long period, while the same behavior from a customer historically purchasing parts or lubricants may indicate a more meaningful change in purchasing behavior.

## Recommendation

Recency and frequency should not be interpreted independently from product behavior.

Low-frequency customers purchasing recurring categories such as parts and lubricants may represent stronger reactivation opportunities than customers whose historical value comes primarily from implements.

